{date:'GOTHAM CITY · 1982',title:'A night at the movies',text:'<p>Gotham’s old theater empties into a rain-bright street. Thomas and Martha Wayne walk home with their young son, Bruce, still talking about the film. His mother holds his hand; his father listens as if the story matters as much as the night is beautiful.</p><p>Their shortcut passes a narrow alley. A stranger steps from the dark and asks for money. Thomas does not reach for a weapon or shout. He gives the gunman <strong>$200</strong>—everything he has—and tries to make himself the only danger in the alley.</p><p class="quote">“Take it. Just let my family go.”</p><p>For one suspended second, the city seems to hold its breath.</p>'},
{date:'GOTHAM CITY · THAT NIGHT',title:'The promise',text:'<p>The gunshot cracks across the street. Thomas and Martha fall beneath the streetlamp. Bruce is left kneeling beside them, surrounded by a city that keeps moving but will never look the same to him again.</p><p>In the weeks that follow, adults speak softly around him. They tell him he is safe now, that the police will find the killer, that time will make the pain smaller. None of those words can bring his parents back. Bruce learns that helplessness can feel louder than any siren.</p><p>Grief hardens into a promise—not a plan yet, only a vow he will carry into every room: <strong>no one else should have to feel this alone.</strong></p>'},
{date:'WAYNE MANOR · THE YEARS AFTER',title:'A house full of echoes',text:'<p>The manor is enormous, but after the funeral it feels too quiet. Bruce grows up among portraits, locked rooms, and the careful kindness of the family’s longtime butler, Alfred Pennyworth. Alfred makes breakfast, remembers birthdays, and never asks Bruce to pretend he is all right.</p><p>Bruce studies, travels, and searches for a way to understand the violence that took his parents. He learns how fear works, how people survive, and how easily a powerful family can mistake its own comfort for the health of its city.</p><p>The promise stays with him. It changes shape as he does: first a child’s wish, then a question, and finally a responsibility he cannot hand to anyone else.</p>'},
{date:'GOTHAM CITY · 2008',title:'A fortune, a house, a name',text:'<p>Twenty-six years later, Bruce uncovers a fortune his father had quietly set aside—seed money for a plan Thomas never finished. Bruce invests it, buys back a neglected mansion, and gives the work a public name: <strong>Wayne Enterprises.</strong></p><p>From the outside, it looks like a return to the life he was born to lead. The grand rooms fill with donors, champagne, and polished promises. Harvey Dent, Gotham’s tireless district attorney, is among the guests. He believes the city can be repaired through courage and honest institutions.</p><p>Bruce admires that conviction. He also knows how many people are waiting for someone else to fix things. Behind the parties, he begins asking harder questions about what a single person can do.</p>'},
