121: {date:'GOTHAM CITY · 2009',title:'The cost of hope',text:'<p>A year later, a terrible incident leaves Harvey Dent’s face scarred and his life changed. The city’s bright public hope is suddenly vulnerable to pain, anger, and the cruel appetite of headlines. Bruce watches the damage from a distance and recognizes the same old feeling: the moment when safety proves it was never guaranteed.</p><p>He retreats from the parties and the easy explanations. With a bag in hand, he descends beneath Wayne Manor into a forgotten cave. The stone is cold, the passages are rough, and the darkness seems almost honest. Here, at least, nobody asks him to smile for a photograph.</p><p>For a while, Bruce tells himself solitude is the same thing as purpose.</p>'},
122: {date:'WAYNE CAVE · THE FIRST VISIT',title:'A man in the dark',text:'<p>“Excuse me, sir. Are you looking for something?” an old man calls from the cave entrance. “There’s nothing in here. It’s old—just like me.”</p><p>Bruce bristles. “I’m trying to do something with this place. What makes you think you can tell me what to do?” The stranger does not rise to the challenge. He simply points out that Bruce is standing on private property and waits for him to answer.</p><p>Bruce refuses to leave. The old man gives him one last look and walks away. The exchange is awkward, almost absurd, but it stays with Bruce. Someone has finally spoken to him plainly, without fear of his name or his money.</p>'},
123: {date:'WAYNE CAVE · THREE MONTHS LATER',title:'The long quiet',text:'<p>Days pass. Bruce returns to the cave with tools, then with plans, then with nothing but questions. He measures the cavern and marks out possible work areas. He tries to turn the rock into a headquarters by force of will, but every task exposes another one he does not know how to do.</p><p>Above him, the manor continues its routines. Below, the cave answers with drips of water and the occasional flutter of wings. Bruce begins to understand that a mission built alone may only become another way to hide.</p><p>One evening he hears footsteps at the entrance. The same old man has come back. This time, he is carrying a thermos and a small stack of notes.</p>'},
124: {date:'WAYNE CAVE · A RETURN',title:'An unexpected ally',text:'<p>“Excuse me, sir. Remember me?” The man sets down the thermos. “I’m Alfred.” Bruce has known him all his life, of course, but down here they meet differently: not as employer and servant, nor as grieving boy and patient guardian, but as two people looking at the same unfinished room.</p><p>Alfred has sketched a workbench, a computer station, and a safer route back to the house. He has also found a book on self-defense. Bruce studies the pages and recognizes how much he does not know. Alfred does not gloat. He pours tea.</p><p>“You’ll need more than determination, sir,” he says. “Determination is what gets you to the door. Preparation is what gets you home.”</p>'},
125: {date:'WAYNE CAVE · THE PLAN TAKES SHAPE',title:'Tools, not answers',text:'<p>They work in fits and starts. Alfred knows how to keep a project moving; Bruce knows how to ask questions that make the project bigger. The computer station becomes a place to sort reports and maps. A suit bay takes shape against the stone. The elevator connects the cave to the estate without sending either man through the front hall.</p><p>Every object has a purpose and a limit. Batarangs can distract or disarm, but they cannot make a bad decision wise. Smoke pellets can cover a retreat, but they cannot turn a retreat into a victory. A grapple prototype can bridge a gap only if the person using it thinks before leaping.</p><p>Bruce begins to see the cave not as a lair, but as a workshop for learning how to help.</p>'},
126: {date:'WAYNE CAVE · THE VEHICLE BAY',title:'A machine with sharp edges',text:'<p>The car is the most ambitious piece of the plan and the least finished. Its armored panels catch the work lights; its engine answers a test with a growl that makes dust fall from the ceiling. Alfred circles it with a clipboard, noting what still needs attention.</p><p>“It’s not quite street legal,” he says. “And I would avoid the red switch.” Bruce asks if it is safe. Alfred considers the question for a beat too long. They agree to call it a prototype and to keep the first test somewhere far from traffic.</p><p>The machine represents possibility, but also temptation: the urge to make an entrance instead of doing the patient work. Bruce closes the hood. Tonight, the car can wait.</p>'},
127: {date:'WAYNE CAVE · BEFORE DAWN',title:'The first lesson',text:'<p>Alfred lays the self-defense field guide on the workbench. It is not as impressive as the suit, the car, or the glowing computer screens. Its pages are worn at the corners, full of diagrams and notes in Alfred’s neat hand. Bruce opens it carefully.</p><p>“Begin with the book,” Alfred tells him. “Learn how to keep your balance. Learn when to move, when to listen, and when walking away is the bravest thing you can do.” Bruce looks toward the equipment waiting around the cave. For the first time, it feels less like a collection of answers and more like a list of responsibilities.</p><p>There is still a long way to go. But the work has finally begun.</p>'},
128: {date:'WAYNE CAVE · NOW',title:'A place to begin',text:'<p>Alfred has left the gear ready: the field guide on the bench, prototypes on the armor table, the suit in its display frame, and the Batmobile resting beneath the stone ceiling. The elevator waits at the far end of the cave. Nothing here is finished, and nobody pretends otherwise.</p><p>Bruce takes in the quiet workshop and the steady lights. Beyond the cave lies a city full of strangers, each with a story he cannot know yet. He cannot undo the night that changed his life. He can choose what he does with the nights still ahead.</p><p class="quote">One man. One promise. A first step into the dark.</p><p>Explore the cave, inspect every piece of equipment, and take the elevator when you are ready.</p>'}
